I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided powder-XRD tooling/reference library first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine them in GSAS-II, validate the quantitative fit, and write all requested artifacts from that final project.

The chemistry is Sr–W–O with possible carbonate carryover at 650 °C, so I’ll treat SrWO₄, residual WO₃/SrCO₃, and Sr-rich intermediates as hypotheses—not assumptions. I’m now extracting peak positions and querying the frozen COD subset for all compatible Sr/W/O/C structures, then I’ll compare full-pattern simulations rather than rely on a few diagnostic lines.

Peak matching supports three phases: scheelite-type SrWO₄ plus monoclinic WO₃ and orthorhombic SrCO₃. The weak lines at about 22.8/23.27/24.08° track the WO₃ triplet, while 24.98/25.55° and associated higher-angle lines track strontianite. I’m now refining these jointly and comparing all plausible WO₃ references; this is also where a hidden fourth phase would become evident in the residual.

The three-phase model is now at Rwp ≈ 4.17% (GOF ≈ 1.05), essentially at the counting-statistics floor of 3.98%. The refined crystalline fractions are stable near 81.3% SrWO₄, 11.7% WO₃, and 7.1% SrCO₃; the WO₃:SrCO₃ residual ratio also matches the precursor stoichiometry. I’m doing the final nuisance/texture checks and physical validation before exporting the deliverables.

